{"subjectId":"python","id":"arguments","title":"Argument Kinds: *args, **kwargs, / and *","group":"Functions","order":24,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A Python signature can say exactly how each parameter may be passed — by position, by name, or either — and can soak up any number of extras with <code>*args</code> and <code>**kwargs</code>.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-numbers\"></i>The full signature, in order</h3>\n<div class=\"formula\"><code>def f(pos_only, /, pos_or_kw, *args, kw_only, **kwargs)</code></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Syntax</th><th>How callers pass it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Positional-only</td><td>before <code>/</code></td><td>Only by position: <code>f(1)</code> — <code>f(a=1)</code> is an error</td></tr><tr><td>Positional-or-keyword</td><td>between <code>/</code> and <code>*</code></td><td>Either: <code>f(1)</code> or <code>f(b=1)</code> — the default kind</td></tr><tr><td>Variadic positional</td><td><code>*args</code></td><td>Extra positional values, collected into a <strong>tuple</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Keyword-only</td><td>after <code>*</code> or <code>*args</code></td><td>Only by name: <code>f(key=1)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Variadic keyword</td><td><code>**kwargs</code></td><td>Extra named values, collected into a <strong>dict</strong> (must be last)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def f(a, b=1, /, c=2, *args, d, e=5, **kwargs):\n    return f\"a={a} b={b} c={c} args={args} d={d} e={e} kwargs={kwargs}\"\n\nprint(f(10, d=4))\n# a=10 b=1 c=2 args=() d=4 e=5 kwargs={}\nprint(f(10, 20, 30, 40, 50, d=4, z=9))\n# a=10 b=20 c=30 args=(40, 50) d=4 e=5 kwargs={'z': 9}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>a=10 b=1 c=2 args=() d=4 e=5 kwargs={}\na=10 b=20 c=30 args=(40, 50) d=4 e=5 kwargs={'z': 9}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Once a parameter has a default, every later <em>positional</em> parameter needs one too. Keyword-only parameters are exempt — <code>d</code> above has no default even though <code>c</code> does.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-key\"></i>Keyword-only parameters (<code>*</code>)</h3>\n<p>A bare <code>*</code> ends the positional parameters. Everything after it must be named at the call site — ideal for flags and options that would be unreadable as bare <code>True</code>/<code>False</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def connect(host, port, *, timeout=10, retries=3):\n    return f\"{host}:{port} t={timeout} r={retries}\"\n\nprint(connect(\"db\", 5432, timeout=2))     # db:5432 t=2 r=3\ntry:\n    connect(\"db\", 5432, 2)                # which option is 2? Python refuses to guess\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(e)   # connect() takes 2 positional arguments but 3 were given</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p><code>sort(items, reverse=True)</code> — boolean and option parameters keyword-only, so call sites explain themselves.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p><code>process(data, True, False, 3)</code> — nobody can read that without opening the definition.</p></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i>Positional-only parameters (<code>/</code>, 3.8+)</h3>\n<p>Parameters before <code>/</code> can't be passed by name. Many built-ins work this way (<code>len(obj=…)</code> is an error). It lets a library rename parameters later without breaking callers, and frees the name for <code>**kwargs</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def distance(x, y, /):\n    return (x * x + y * y) ** 0.5\n\nprint(distance(3, 4))                  # 5.0\ntry:\n    distance(x=3, y=4)\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(type(e).__name__)            # TypeError\n\ndef format_row(name, /, **fields):     # 'name' can ALSO appear as a field\n    return f\"{name}: {fields}\"\n\nprint(format_row(\"user\", name=\"asha\"))  # user: {'name': 'asha'}</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets\"></i><code>*args</code> and <code>**kwargs</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def total(*nums):\n    return sum(nums)\n\ndef tag(name, **attrs):\n    parts = \" \".join(f'{k}=\"{v}\"' for k, v in attrs.items())\n    return f\"&lt;{name} {parts}&gt;\"\n\nprint(total(), total(1, 2, 3))                 # 0 6\nprint(tag(\"a\", href=\"/home\", id=\"nav\"))        # &lt;a href=\"/home\" id=\"nav\"&gt;</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>The names <code>args</code> and <code>kwargs</code> are only convention — the <code>*</code> and <code>**</code> do the work. Use descriptive names when they help: <code>*paths</code>, <code>**options</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-maximize\"></i>Unpacking at the call site</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def point(x, y, z=0):\n    return (x, y, z)\n\ncoords = [1, 2]\nopts = {\"z\": 9}\nprint(point(*coords))                  # (1, 2, 0)\nprint(point(*coords, **opts))          # (1, 2, 9)\nprint(point(**{\"x\": 5, \"y\": 6}))       # (5, 6, 0)\n\ndefaults = {\"z\": 1}\noverrides = {\"z\": 3}\nprint(point(0, 0, **{**defaults, **overrides}))   # (0, 0, 3)   ← merge first, later wins\nprint(max(*[4, 8, 2]), *\"abc\")         # 8 a b c</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Passing the same keyword twice is an error, even through unpacking: <code>point(1, 2, z=0, **{\"z\": 9})</code> raises <code>TypeError: got multiple values for keyword argument 'z'</code>. Merge the dicts first.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def point(x, y, z=0):\n    return (x, y, z)\ntry:\n    point(1, 2, z=0, **{\"z\": 9})\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(type(e).__name__, \"multiple values\" in str(e))   # TypeError True\ntry:\n    point(1, x=1)\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(e)   # point() got multiple values for argument 'x'</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-forward\"></i>Forwarding arguments</h3>\n<p><code>*args, **kwargs</code> in a wrapper accepts <em>anything</em> and passes it on unchanged — the core trick behind decorators, proxies and subclass constructors.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import functools\n\ndef logged(func):\n    @functools.wraps(func)\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        shown = [repr(a) for a in args] + [f\"{k}={v!r}\" for k, v in kwargs.items()]\n        print(f\"{func.__name__}({', '.join(shown)})\")\n        return func(*args, **kwargs)\n    return wrapper\n\n@logged\ndef power(base, exp=2):\n    return base ** exp\n\nprint(power(3, exp=3))</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>power(3, exp=3)\n27</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>Inspecting a signature</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import inspect\n\ndef f(a, /, b, *args, c, d=1, **kw):\n    pass\n\nsig = inspect.signature(f)\nprint(str(sig))                  # (a, /, b, *args, c, d=1, **kw)\nprint([p.kind.name for p in sig.parameters.values()])\n# ['POSITIONAL_ONLY', 'POSITIONAL_OR_KEYWORD', 'VAR_POSITIONAL', 'KEYWORD_ONLY', 'KEYWORD_ONLY', 'VAR_KEYWORD']\nbound = sig.bind(1, 2, 3, c=4)\nprint(bound.arguments)           # {'a': 1, 'b': 2, 'args': (3,), 'c': 4}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>(a, /, b, *args, c, d=1, **kw)\n['POSITIONAL_ONLY', 'POSITIONAL_OR_KEYWORD', 'VAR_POSITIONAL', 'KEYWORD_ONLY', 'KEYWORD_ONLY', 'VAR_KEYWORD']\n{'a': 1, 'b': 2, 'args': (3,), 'c': 4}</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Defaults are evaluated once</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import time\n\ndef stamp(when=time.time()):      # evaluated ONCE, at def time\n    return when\n\nprint(stamp() == stamp())         # True   ← same frozen value every call\n\ndef stamp_ok(when=None):\n    return time.time() if when is None else when</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Same rule as the mutable-default trap: use <code>None</code> as a sentinel and compute the real default inside the function. If <code>None</code> is a valid value, use a private sentinel: <code>_MISSING = object()</code>.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What are *args and **kwargs?</summary><div><p>In a definition, <code>*args</code> collects extra positional arguments into a tuple and <code>**kwargs</code> collects extra keyword arguments into a dict. In a call, <code>*seq</code> and <code>**mapping</code> do the reverse — unpack into separate arguments.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What do / and * mean in a parameter list?</summary><div><p>Parameters before <code>/</code> are positional-only; parameters after <code>*</code> (or after <code>*args</code>) are keyword-only. So in <code>def f(a, /, b, *, c)</code>, <code>a</code> must be positional, <code>c</code> must be named, and <code>b</code> can be either.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why make parameters keyword-only?</summary><div><p>Readability and safety: <code>resize(img, 100, 200, True)</code> becomes <code>resize(img, width=100, height=200, keep_ratio=True)</code>, arguments can't be swapped by accident, and you can add or reorder options later without breaking callers.</p></div></details></section>"}